WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
A frog egg divides into thousands of cells without growing at all.

2
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.700
So where do the cells come from, and what does each one get?

3
00:00:12.783 --> 00:00:14.943
Development starts at fertilization.

4
00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:20.103
A sperm fuses with the egg, and a wave of calcium crosses it.

5
00:00:21.143 --> 00:00:25.583
A frog egg was paused partway through meiosis, and the calcium wakes it.

6
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:30.043
Only one sperm should get in, and the egg keeps the rest out.

7
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:35.313
Next comes cleavage, a run of divisions with no growth between them.

8
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.493
Each new cell is called a blastomere.

9
00:00:39.593 --> 00:00:42.053
One cell becomes two, then four, then eight.

10
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.627
Watch the outline.

11
00:00:46.927 --> 00:00:48.087
It never gets bigger.

12
00:00:49.387 --> 00:00:52.387
Each division cuts the same egg into more, smaller pieces.

13
00:00:53.807 --> 00:00:55.567
The egg is divided, not grown.

14
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.987
Suppose every division is equal.

15
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.887
After five divisions there are thirty-two cells.

16
00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:06.927
Is each cell one thirty-second as wide as the egg?

17
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:19.303
No. In this equal model, each cell holds one thirty-second of the volume.

18
00:01:20.423 --> 00:01:24.923
But volume is shared out in three dimensions: length, width and depth.

19
00:01:26.143 --> 00:01:29.023
So each cell is about a third as wide as the egg.

20
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.587
Something else has changed.

21
00:01:32.787 --> 00:01:35.947
There are now thirty-two nuclei, but no new cytoplasm.

22
00:01:37.307 --> 00:01:39.987
Each nucleus has far less cytoplasm around it.

23
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:44.377
Now suppose the egg is not the same all through.

24
00:01:45.777 --> 00:01:47.097
Mark one region in purple.

25
00:01:48.337 --> 00:01:52.317
This stands for inherited material, placed in the egg before it divides.

26
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.280
Suppose the purple is anchored, so it stays put.

27
00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.680
The furrows cut through the egg around it.

28
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.620
After a few divisions, only some cells hold it.

29
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.840
Their neighbours have none.

30
00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:10.723
That is what a cell inherits: the slice of egg it was cut from.

31
00:02:11.963 --> 00:02:16.583
If an anchored molecule helps set fate, it is a cytoplasmic determinant.

32
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:18.977
Yolk changes the cutting.

33
00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:22.837
Yolk is stored food, and it slows the furrows.

34
00:02:24.037 --> 00:02:27.977
A frog egg divides completely, with bigger cells in its yolky half.

35
00:02:29.297 --> 00:02:31.977
A chick divides only a disc on top of its yolk.

36
00:02:33.157 --> 00:02:38.637
A fly first divides its nuclei in shared cytoplasm, with no membranes between them.

37
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.350
Edwin Conklin watched the egg of a sea squirt.

38
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:47.030
Part of its cytoplasm is yellow, and it gathers into a crescent.

39
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.710
He followed it cell by cell.

40
00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.410
It ended up mainly in the cells that build the larva's tail muscle.

41
00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:00.143
But following a colour shows where it goes, not what it does.

42
00:03:01.403 --> 00:03:03.883
Does the crescent make muscle, or just ride along?

43
00:03:05.043 --> 00:03:06.443
That takes a different test.

44
00:03:07.483 --> 00:03:09.723
So cleavage shares out one fixed egg.

45
00:03:11.003 --> 00:03:14.643
Each cell gets its own slice, and anchored material comes with it.
